From d54a2768051b1591d24af0dd19151d53a86c6760 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andy1210 <andy1210@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] scene: offload the top surface to an output layer

The renderer is all-or-nothing: one surface above a fullscreen video sends the
whole output through it. At 4K that is a full-screen composite per frame for
something display hardware can do for free - the case is exactly what overlay
planes exist for.

When the render list holds a surface covering the output and exactly one above
it, hand the top one to the backend as a wlr_output_layer and direct-scan-out
the one below. The backend answers after the commit, per the wlr_output_layer
contract, so the verdict for a frame is the one the previous commit gave; a
refusal backs off rather than giving up, because what was refused was the
buffer, not the arrangement.

Measured on a Raspberry Pi 5 (vc4) with a 4K film under a fullscreen
translucent UI: the compositor GPU time goes from 67% to 0% and the player
stops dropping frames.
